course: myths
lesson: 3
title: Eyes and ears
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Grandma pushes the carrots across the table: "Eat up, they'll help you see in the dark!" She heard it as a child in the war, when everyone said British night pilots shot down enemy planes thanks to carrots. The real secret was radar, and the carrot story helped hide it.
explain: Eye and ear myths are everywhere: square eyes from sitting close to the telly, eyes stuck crossed when the wind changes, glasses that make eyes lazy, torches under the covers that ruin your sight, blind people with super ears.
explain: Most of these are harmless, and a few hide a real truth: dim light just makes eyes tired, carrots' vitamin A keeps healthy eyes working (but gives no night vision), and blind people often use sound with great skill they have trained.
explain: But two myths can really hurt: looking at the Sun during an eclipse can burn the back of your eye without any pain, and loud music can damage your hearing without hurting, and that damage does not grow back.
explain: So laugh at the jokes, enjoy the war story, and when a myth could hurt a friend, speak up kindly but firmly.
fact: Reading in dim light or under the covers with a torch can make eyes tired or give a headache, but it does not permanently harm them.
fact: Carrots contain vitamin A, which healthy eyes need, but eating extra carrots does not give you night vision.
fact: In the Second World War, Britain spread the carrot story to help hide that its pilots used radar.
fact: Sitting close to a modern screen does not damage your eyes (the warning began with very old TVs, which gave off a little radiation); lots of screen time and little time outdoors are linked to short sight in children.
fact: Crossing your eyes for a moment is harmless; the eye muscles relax back to normal, and the wind changes nothing.
fact: Glasses do not make eyes weaker or lazy; children's short sight often gets worse as they grow, with or without glasses.
fact: Earwax protects and cleans your ears by itself; doctors say not to push cotton buds inside, as they can push wax deeper or hurt the eardrum, and the packets themselves warn not to put them inside the ear.
fact: Many blind people use sounds more skilfully, partly because the brain rewires and attention is trained; their ears themselves are not stronger.
fact: Looking at the Sun during an eclipse is not safe: even a sliver can burn the retina (the seeing layer at the back of the eye), and it does not hurt while it happens; use proper eclipse glasses or a pinhole viewer.
fact: Long exposure to loud sound can damage hearing without any pain, and damaged hearing does not grow back.
fact: Squinting briefly makes the view sharper and does not damage eyes; needing to squint is a sign to get an eye test.
fact: Eye tests are free for children in the UK, and an optician can answer most eye myths.
fact: Two things happening together, such as new glasses and worse sight, does not mean one caused the other.
exercise: What does reading under the covers with a torch really do to your eyes? | It can make them tired, but does no lasting harm | It ruins your eyesight forever after just one night | It makes your eyes see much better in the dark later on | It turns the whites of your eyes red for a whole week
exercise: Why did Britain spread the carrot story in the Second World War? | To hide that its pilots used radar | To sell more carrots to farmers | Because scientists had proved it true | To stop children eating too many sweets
exercise: What does vitamin A from carrots really do? | It helps healthy eyes work normally | It gives you night vision like a cat | It makes your eyes change colour | It lets you see through walls
exercise: Does sitting close to a modern telly damage your eyes? | No, but lots of screen time and little outdoor time are linked to short sight | Yes, it makes eyes go square | Yes, it burns the back of the eye | No, and sitting close actually improves eyesight
exercise: You cross your eyes and the wind changes. What happens? | Your eyes go back to normal when you stop | Your eyes stay crossed for ever | Your eyes stay crossed until the next wind change | Only one eye goes back to normal
exercise: Uncle Raj says glasses make your eyes lazy. What is true? | Glasses don't weaken eyes; they help you see clearly | Glasses make your eyes a little weaker every year you wear them | Glasses cure short sight after a few months | Glasses only work if you wear them in bed
exercise: What does earwax do? | It protects and cleans your ears | It shows your ears are dirty | It makes you hear less on purpose | It is old soap stuck from the bath
exercise: Why do doctors say not to push cotton buds into your ears? | They can push wax deeper or hurt the eardrum | The cotton makes long hairs grow inside your ears | Cotton buds are made of a poison that seeps into ears | They make your ears taste bad if you lick your finger
exercise: Why can many blind people pick out sounds so well? | They practise using sound, and the brain rewires | Their ears are physically stronger and bigger than other people's | They were all born with super hearing | They wear secret hearing machines
exercise: Is it safe to stare at the Sun during an eclipse because the sky is dim? | No, even a sliver can burn the retina without pain | Yes, the dark makes it completely safe | Yes, if you only stare for one minute | No, but sunglasses make it perfectly safe
exercise: Which is a safe way to watch an eclipse? | A pinhole viewer made from card | Ordinary sunglasses | A photo negative held to your eye | Squinting through your fingers
exercise: Harry says loud music only hurts your ears if it actually hurts. What is true? | Hearing can be damaged with no pain at all | Ears always hurt before any damage happens | Loud music makes ears stronger over time | Only loud sounds at night cause damage
exercise: Can damaged hearing grow back? | No, it does not grow back | Yes, after a good night's sleep | Yes, if you eat carrots | Yes, after about a month of quiet
exercise: What does needing to squint usually tell you? | It's time for an eye test | Your eyes are being damaged by squinting | You are about to sneeze | Your eyes need more carrots
exercise: Uncle Raj's sight got worse after he got glasses. Why doesn't that prove glasses did it? | Sight often changes anyway; together is not the same as cause | Because his glasses were the wrong colour | Because glasses only work in the morning | It does prove it; one thing after another is how science works
exercise: Lily's granny says the wind will fix her crossed eyes. What is the best way to react? | Laugh along; it is a teasing warning | Never pull a face again, just in case | Check the weather forecast before blinking | Tell Granny she is being dangerous
test: "eclipse is today, it's dark so just look at it, innit" says Ava. What do you say? | Please don't, it can burn your eyes; let's make a pinhole viewer | Fine, but only look for ten seconds | Look at it through a glass of water | Wear two pairs of sunglasses and look
test: Your mate has headphones on full and says, "Shut up, it doesn't hurt, so it's fine." What do you tell him? | Ears can be damaged without hurting, and it doesn't heal | He's right; if there's no pain then there's no damage at all | It's only a problem for children if they are under five years old | Music can't damage ears, only very loud machines like drills can
test: What would happen if you ate a whole bag of carrots before a night walk? | You'd see in the dark no better than usual | You'd see in the dark like an owl | You'd glow a little orange | You'd see in the dark but only for an hour
test: Dad says you'll go square-eyed sitting near the telly. Where might the old warning come from? | Very old TVs gave off a little radiation, and the warning stayed | Scientists found children with square eyes in a famous study in 1990 | Modern TVs pull eyes into a square shape | Square eyes are common in people who watch cartoons
test: Freya cleans deep inside her ears with cotton buds every day and shows off about it. What do you point out? | The packet itself says not to put them in your ear | She should use two cotton buds instead of one | Wax only comes out if you never wash your hair with shampoo | Cotton buds should be wet to work properly
test: "bet blind people have bionic ears, like Daredevil" says Noah after the film. Which answer is fairest? | Partly true: many train great skill with sound, not stronger ears | Totally true: their ears are made of metal | Totally false: blind people hear worse than others | Only blind superheroes hear better, nobody else
test: Chloe says, "Stop squinting, you'll wreck your eyes!" What's the truth? | Squinting doesn't harm eyes; it's a sign to get them tested | She's right, squinting ruins eyes quickly | Squinting is a cure for short sight if you practise it every day | Squinting only harms you at school
test: Mum says stop reading with the torch, you'll ruin your eyes. What's a wise reply? | "I'll stop because it's bedtime, but it only makes them tired." | "No, reading in the dark makes eyes stronger." | "Then I'll read with no torch at all." | "Eyes can't get tired, Mum, that's a myth."
test: Which of these eye and ear myths is most dangerous to believe? | Staring at an eclipse is safe because it's dark | Carrots will help you to see in the dark like a cat | You'll go square-eyed if you sit too near the telly | Your eyes will stay crossed for good if the wind changes
test: In a cartoon, a boy wears glasses for a day and his eyes get "lazy" and drop shut. What's wrong? | Glasses don't make eyes lazy; they help you see clearly | Nothing, glasses do that to everyone | It should take a whole week of wearing them, not a single day | Only sunglasses make eyes lazy
exam: Identify the principal reason for which the notion that carrots confer night vision was disseminated in wartime Britain. | To conceal the employment of radar by its pilots | To augment the sales of agricultural produce such as carrots | To publicise a novel medical discovery concerning vision | To discourage the consumption of confectionery by children
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the observation of the Sun during an eclipse? | It is hazardous, the retina being liable to injury without sensation of pain | It is safe, the diminished light precluding injury | It is safe provided ordinary sunglasses are worn | It is hazardous solely to persons of advanced age
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning prolonged exposure to loud sound. | Hearing may be impaired without pain, and such impairment is permanent | Injury to hearing invariably occasions pain | Impaired hearing is restored in full after a sufficient period of quiet | Prolonged exposure fortifies the organ of hearing
exam: Ascertain the effect upon the eyes of the habitual wearing of spectacles by a short-sighted child. | Vision is rendered clear, and the eyes are not weakened | The eyes are progressively weakened and rendered idle by their use | The short sight is permanently cured within months | The eyes become dependent and cease to focus
